        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-05T10:26:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This feels odd, shouldn\u0027t the hardware do this automatically for us instead of having to manually do this?",
      "revId": "65512cf8a0825f45acbcd65b6865834ca41fb6d2",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c3231024_a0f23df7",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-05T10:27:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In other words, this should \"just happen\" by the hardware no matter what, it should not be needed to be set manually here, otherwise how would you know to, or not to, set this value?",
      "parentUuid": "9a1659d7_f179ad94",
      "revId": "65512cf8a0825f45acbcd65b6865834ca41fb6d2",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9c1c99f7_e56c1d2e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1981633
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-05T15:44:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My understanding is that the hardware handles appending the packet (when req-\u003ezero is set), but it is ultimately up to the driver to let the hardware know that the transfer is complete with this request (by setting req-\u003ezero). Otherwise, the hardware would just append the zlp every time the request is full.\n\nThe other examples we have in android using function fs (adb, mtp, and ptp) all use the manual zlp workaround (aka sending an addtional 0 byte request to the kernel to indicate it should issue a zlp). \n\nWe\u0027re running into issues with this manual workaround because we have to maintain compatibility with the existing downstream accessory driver. The accessory driver exposes a single character file for read and write, while function fs exposes separate endpoint files - one for read, one for write. To provide app compatibility, we have added a \u0027legacy bridge\u0027 which wraps these two EP fds in a single socket which is exposed to the userspace app.\n\nThis legacy bridge app is where we are handling the ZLP, and ultimately, the addition of the workaround ZLP packet causes the userspace APP to see a separate transaction for the ZLP. This is compatibility breaking, meaning our userspace AOA implementation is not a drop in replacement.\n\nWe\u0027ve explored other approaches, but ultimately they added significant overhead causing reduced bandwidth through the legacy bridge.",
      "parentUuid": "c3231024_a0f23df7",
      "revId": "65512cf8a0825f45acbcd65b6865834ca41fb6d2",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "87d855c5_ba08c367",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-06T06:28:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But the hardware is supposed to send a ZLP every time the request is \"full\", that\u0027s how USB works here, right?  This should be transparent to userspace, you shouldn\u0027t have to manually set this to happen or not, the hardware should just do it automatically.\n\nHow can userspace see an extra transaction for ZLP, the hardware should just \"eat\" that as it now knows the buffer is completed and so the data should be sent further up the stack.  WHat host side software is seeing extra transactions like this?",
      "parentUuid": "9c1c99f7_e56c1d2e",
      "revId": "65512cf8a0825f45acbcd65b6865834ca41fb6d2",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
